extra='''<section><h2>Failure mechanisms and data needed</h2><table><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Evidence and boundary</th><th>Proposed data</th></tr><tr><td>Wrong rule-table lookup</td><td>Two errors in one tax task. Only the bracket error changes the reported tax.</td><td>Dated tables, boundary cases, selected-row rationale and independent calculations.</td></tr><tr><td>Eligibility condition omitted</td><td>One earlier payroll task, tested with changed inputs. No actual payment established.</td><td>Eligible and ineligible combinations, approval conditions, executable expected outcomes.</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty omitted from review handoff</td><td>TAX-06 flags an unclassified source field; its financial effect is unresolved and not human-adjudicated.</td><td>Source gaps, explicit review holds and expert decisions about what can proceed.</td></tr><tr><td>Incomplete work accepted by a judge</td><td>Shipping forecast remains missing and routing is on HOLD. Restricted research tools confound solver attribution.</td><td>Complete, honestly incomplete and fabricated deliverables with completion labels.</td></tr></table><p>Printing fidelity, conflicting sources, rubric over-scope and provider errors are tracked separately. This is a small diagnostic taxonomy; it is not thirty independently confirmed failures.</p></section>
<section><h2>Two lab proposals</h2><h3>OpenAI</h3><p>For Turing’s professional-work dataset, I propose an expert-reviewed rule-application pilot. The tax case shows how a wrong upstream rule survives consistent downstream arithmetic. Gold native files can support supervised fine-tuning, minimal corrections can form preference pairs, and executable calculations can provide verifiable rewards. First compare your grader with expert adjudication on held-out families; training benefit remains a hypothesis.</p><h3>Anthropic</h3><p>I propose a separate workflow-control pilot focused on eligibility, approval dependencies and appropriate uncertainty. Claude’s tax review found the right error but its quotation was not grounded. Start by running Claude as a solver on held-out workflows, then test condition preservation, unnecessary holds and citation fidelity with expert labels and executable state checks. This extension evaluated Claude as a judge, so it does not establish a Claude solver weakness.</p></section>
<section><h2>What remains unfinished</h2><p>Official-rubric regrading; a blinded specialist review sample; completion of the additional judge batch; native Excel reproduction of the print issue; and any training intervention. Four outputs currently have valid records from both new judges. Judge agreement cannot establish accuracy.</p><p>The project rubrics were written with a coding agent and emphasize inspectable task requirements. This choice makes local mechanisms explicit but weakens comparability with official scoring. The saved pinned export already contains human-authored rubric fields. Their availability must not be described as a reason for using custom rubrics.</p><p><a href="https://evalscope.readthedocs.io/en/latest/benchmarks/gdpval.html">EvalScope grading documentation</a> documents the rubric fields and external quality grading. The dataset revision is <code>11e7900cdcac61bc4daf59e65feb238acda98fbf</code>. The 95% interval describes binomial uncertainty within the selected cohort; it does not make the purposive sample representative.</p></section>'''
